Arcade-CrystalCastles_MiSTer: fix the vertical cocktail flip

For upstream (github.com/MiSTer-devel/Arcade-CrystalCastles_MiSTer), against
the core's known issue: "In cocktail mode the player 2 upside down screen and
sprites are not positioned correctly."

WHY
---
With PLAYER2 set, the vertical scan counter is reloaded from the scroll
register and counts *down* from it, so visible line n shows bitmap row
vr - n. A screen flip has to show the same 232 rows in reverse, which is
vr + 231 - n. The two differ by 25 rows, and the vs floor at CCastles.v:201
then smears the 25 lines that fall off the bottom of the count into a band
of one repeated row across the top of the flipped picture.

The game cannot compensate and does not try. CIN.MAC adjusts the horizontal
scroll for the flip (ORA #3) and the vertical scroll not at all, so the
vertical mirror has to be the hardware's own work. Motion objects are not
affected: the hardware mirrors each object's picture within itself, while
CWV.MAC's EN.PMV mirrors the objects' coordinates in software. So the objects
land correctly and the background does not, which is what makes it look like
a sprite-positioning fault.

MEASURED
--------
On the verilated core, driven by a fixture that plants bitmap markers and
motion objects at known places and then sets PLAYER2 mid-run. Flipping maps a
block spanning [t,b] to [C-b, C-t]; bitmap and objects must agree on C:

                                    vertical   horizontal
    motion objects                     233        259
    bitmap, before                     256        255
    bitmap, after this patch           231        255

231 is what a half-turn of the 232 visible lines requires. The change takes
the disagreement from 23 lines to 2.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT FIX
----------------------------
The 2 lines and 4 pixels left over are not the flip's doing. They are twice a
one-line and two-column phase difference between the bitmap scan and the
motion-object pipeline -- the object line buffer is filled on one line and
shown on the next, and the first emitted column is bitmap column hs+2 where
MAME has hs+0. A delay does not mirror, so the flip counts it twice. Both are
invisible upright, which is how they survived; they are also the "Check sprite
horizontal location on actual hardware" item already on the core's own list,
and settling them needs a board, not a simulation.

--- a/rtl/CCastles.v
+++ b/rtl/CCastles.v
@@ -185,7 +185,7 @@
         vr <= #1 BD;
    else
       if (VBLANK)
-         vi <= vr;
+         vi <= PLAYER2 ? vr + 8'd231 : vr;
       else
       begin
          HSYNCd <= #1 HSYNC;
